WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:02.500
Hey, it's Bryan from Oxide back with another FAQ Friday.

2
00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:06.020
And a question we get is, where are the power supplies in the system?

3
00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:10.600
You keep saying there's a DC bus bar that runs DC up and down the rack.

4
00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.080
And I know that sleds blind mate into that DC bus bar.

5
00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.660
But who does the actual conversion from AC to DC?

6
00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:18.360
Where does that happen?

7
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.120
The answer is, that happens here.

8
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.660
That happens in the power shelf.

9
00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.880
The power shelf is a series of rectifiers.

10
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.700
And when we were looking at this, one of the very important things for us is,

11
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.759
finding the right partner for our rectifiers.

12
00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:34.360
What were we looking for in a partner?

13
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:36.040
Well, one, we of course want one

14
00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.860
that made a high quality power supply.

15
00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.800
This is Murata is our PSU partner.

16
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.700
We of course wanted one that had a high quality unit,

17
00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.040
which this absolutely is.

18
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.560
But the other thing that we wanted to be able to do

19
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.600
is really build in in-depth monitoring

20
00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:54.640
of these power supply units.

21
00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.800
And so in particular, we were looking for a partner

22
00:00:58.539 --> 00:01:02.100
around which we could develop really custom software and hardware.

23
00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.780
So what we actually developed is,

24
00:01:04.839 --> 00:01:09.320
in addition to using the Murata power shelf and the power supplies,

25
00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:11.600
we developed our own power shelf controller.

26
00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.740
Power shelf controller plugs into this PMBus interface

27
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.100
and is able to speak to these power supply units.

28
00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.540
And this thing has our service processor on it

29
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.200
and is over our management network.

30
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.119
It allows us to integrate deeply with the Murata power supply

31
00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.180
so we can do things like manage its firmware.

32
00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.120
There's firmware running on these things.

33
00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.740
These things are really sophisticated units.

34
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.480
And we have had issues where in order to be able to deliver what a customer needed,

35
00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.060
we needed to be able to update the firmware on these things.

36
00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.600
That firmware update is handled by this power shelf controller,

37
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.700
and our partnership with Murata allows us to do it.

38
00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.900
It's another example, another concrete example,

39
00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:53.480
of the power of hardware software co-design that we have here at Oxide.

40
00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:54.900
Thanks, and see you next time.
